  "notes": "West leads the ♠K against 7♣. West's lead of the ♠K goes to the ♠A. West is marked with the ♠Q. South's only chance is to play East for the ♠10. In that case, South will be able to execute a double trump squeeze.\n\nFirst, 4 rounds of trumps are played. The ♥2 is discarded from the South hand on the fourth round of clubs. The diamond suit is cashed next. On the last diamond, a spade pitch from West will allow South to ruff out the ♠Q and return to the ♥A to cash the ♠J. West must part with a heart and East is similarily squeezed.\n\nEast now has the sole defensive guard in hearts. East must pitch a spade. South now plays the ♠J, covered and ruffed. When East's ♠10 is pinned, the ♠9 is high and the ♥A can be used to reach it.",
